# M29 — rule 9b's REGISTRY-COLUMN check made unreachable.
#
# WHY THIS MUTATION EXISTS AT ALL: because M20 could not prove this branch, and said so.
#
# M20 replaces rule 9b's `if n <> 1 then` with `if false then`, disabling the sentinel COUNT
# leg. Rule 9b has three branches and only one of them reads that count, so M20 named a
# control it was never able to kill and the gate reported UNATTRIBUTED. Removing the name
# from M20 without covering the branch would have been editing a catcher list until the gate
# went green — the one move that makes the whole mechanism worthless. This is the cover.
#
# WHAT THE BRANCH IS FOR. `public.posture_sentinel_row` holds IDENTIFIERS, interpolated
# through `format('%I')`. If a registered column stopped existing — a rename, a dropped
# column — a rule that trusted the registry would raise 42703 from inside its own EXECUTE and
# be reported as "sentinel rule could not be evaluated", which reads as a harness fault
# rather than as a missing sentinel. So 9b checks the column against pg_attribute FIRST.
#
# `elsif false and not exists (...)` rather than deleting the branch: the mutation must be
# unmatchable, not invalid, and the applier rejects an empty @@REPLACE (M22). With the branch
# unreachable, a registry row naming a nonexistent column falls through to the counting
# branch, whose EXECUTE then raises — so the control's own assertion that `rule9Broken` is
# ABSENT is what goes red. That is the control working exactly as its comment says it should.
id: M29
title: rule 9b's registry-column catalog check made unreachable, so a stale registry row reports as a harness fault
expect: caught
proves: rule 9b validates the sentinel registry against pg_attribute before trusting it, so a renamed or dropped column is reported as a registry error and not as "could not be evaluated"
expect-test: [M29] raises ST099 for a registry row naming a column that does not exist

file: supabase/migrations/20261021140500_verify_security_posture.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
      elsif not exists (select 1 from pg_attribute a
@@REPLACE
      elsif false and not exists (select 1 from pg_attribute a
@@END
